<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-markov-chains" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Markov Chains and Steady States</title>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-markov-motivation">
    <title>A First Example</title>

    <example xml:id="eg-alice-lunch">
      <title>Alice's Lunch</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Alice always eats lunch either at restaurant <m>A</m> or restaurant <m>B</m>.
          Suppose that
          <ul>
            <li><p>Initially she is equally likely to eat at each restaurant.</p></li>
            <li><p>She never eats at <m>A</m> two days in a row.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                If she eats at restaurant <m>B</m> one day, then the next day she is three
                times as likely to eat at <m>B</m> as at <m>A</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
          Calculate the probabilities of her eating at <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> for the first
          week. What do you notice?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The initial state vector is
          <m>\vec{S}_0 = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{1}{2} \\ \frac{1}{2} \end{bmatrix}</m>, and
          the <term>transition matrix</term> is
          <md>
            T = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp \frac{1}{4} \\ 1 \amp \frac{3}{4} \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          where the columns are indexed by today's restaurant (<m>A</m>, then <m>B</m>)
          and the rows by the next day's.
        </p>
        <p>
          Iterating <m>\vec{S}_{k+1} = T\vec{S}_k</m>,
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \vec{S}_1 \amp = T\vec{S}_0 = \begin{bmatrix} 0.125 \\ 0.875 \end{bmatrix},
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \vec{S}_2 \amp = T\vec{S}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 0.2188 \\ 0.7812 \end{bmatrix},
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp \;\; \vdots
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \vec{S}_6 \amp = T\vec{S}_5 = \begin{bmatrix} 0.2001 \\ 0.7999 \end{bmatrix},
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \vec{S}_7 \amp = T\vec{S}_6 = \begin{bmatrix} 0.2001 \\ 0.7999 \end{bmatrix}.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The state vectors seem to be converging to
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0.2 \\ 0.8 \end{bmatrix}</m>. Also, we tried changing the
          initial state vector and it seemed that the transition matrix above causes the
          state vectors to converge to <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0.2 \\ 0.8 \end{bmatrix}</m>
          independent of the choice of <m>\vec{S}_0</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <remark xml:id="rem-steady-state-questions">
      <title>Steady State</title>
      <p>
        Sometimes, as in the example of the customer who ate lunch in restaurant <m>A</m>
        or <m>B</m>, the state vectors converge to a particular vector, called the
        <term>steady state</term> vector.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>How do we know if a Markov chain has a steady state vector?</p></li>
          <li><p>If a Markov chain has a steady state vector, how do we find it?</p></li>
        </ol>
        First we need to learn about regular and stochastic matrices.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-regular-stochastic">
    <title>Regular and Stochastic Matrices</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-regular-matrix">
      <title>Regular Matrix</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The matrix <m>P</m> is a <term>regular matrix</term> if, for some integer
          <m>k \gt 0</m>, all entries of <m>P^k</m> are strictly positive.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="eg-regular">
      <title>A Regular Matrix</title>
      <p>
        <m>T = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp \frac{1}{4} \\ 1 \amp \frac{3}{4} \end{bmatrix}</m>
        is a regular matrix, since
        <md>
          T^2 = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{1}{4} \amp \frac{3}{16} \\ \frac{3}{4} \amp \frac{13}{16} \end{bmatrix}
        </md>
        and all the entries of <m>T^2</m> are positive.
      </p>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-not-regular">
      <title>A Matrix That Is Not Regular</title>
      <p>
        <m>P = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> is not regular,
        since <m>P^2 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>,
        <m>P^3 = P</m>, <m>P^4 = I</m>, and so on. In other words,
        <md>
          P^n = \begin{cases} P \amp n \text{ odd} \\ I \amp n \text{ even,} \end{cases}
        </md>
        hence <m>P^n</m> always has zeros as its entries.
      </p>
    </example>

    <definition xml:id="def-stochastic-matrix">
      <title>Stochastic Matrix</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A square matrix <m>P</m> is a <term>stochastic matrix</term> if its columns are
          probability vectors, i.e., each column has nonnegative entries summing to
          <m>1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <remark xml:id="rem-general-2x2-stochastic">
      <p>
        As an example, consider a <m>2 \times 2</m> stochastic matrix whose entries are
        all nonzero:
        <md>
          T = \begin{bmatrix} 1 - p \amp q \\ p \amp 1 - q \end{bmatrix},
          \qquad \text{where } 0 \lt p, q \lt 1.
        </md>
      </p>
    </remark>

    <example xml:id="eg-stochastic-preserves-sum">
      <title>Stochastic Matrices Preserve Sums</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that stochastic matrices (as defined above) preserve the sum of entries
          when acting on vectors in <m>\R^n</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Assume <m>T</m> is a stochastic matrix and
          <m>\vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \\ \vdots \\ x_n \end{bmatrix} \in \R^n</m>.
          Then the <m>i</m>th entry of <m>T\vec{x}</m> is
          <md>
            \left( T\vec{x} \right)_i = \sum_{j=1}^{n} T_{ij} x_j,
          </md>
          and so
          <md>
            \text{sum of entries of } T\vec{x}
            = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \sum_{j=1}^{n} T_{ij} x_j
            = \sum_{j=1}^{n} \underbrace{\left( \sum_{i=1}^{n} T_{ij} \right)}_{= \, 1} x_j
            = \sum_{j=1}^{n} x_j,
          </md>
          the sum of entries of <m>\vec{x}</m>. The inner sum equals one since <m>T</m> is
          stochastic.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-stochastic-eigenvalue-one">
      <title>Every Stochastic Matrix Has Eigenvalue One</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Show that every stochastic matrix has eigenvalue one.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(I)</term> Note that
          <m>\vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ \vdots \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> is an
          eigenvector of <m>A^T</m> with eigenvalue one. For example, with
          <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp \frac{1}{4} \\ 1 \amp \frac{3}{4} \end{bmatrix}</m>,
          <md>
            A^T \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ \frac{1}{4} \amp \frac{3}{4} \end{bmatrix}
              \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            = (1) \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          Note that each entry of
          <m>A^T \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ \vdots \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> is equal to the sum of
          entries in a column of <m>A</m>, and since the columns of <m>A</m> are
          probability vectors, they add up to one.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(II)</term> Also,
          <m>\det(A - \lambda I) = \det \left( (A - \lambda I)^T \right) = \det(A^T - \lambda I)</m>,
          which means <m>A</m> and <m>A^T</m> have the same eigenvalues.
        </p>
        <p>
          From (I) and (II): <m>A</m> has eigenvalue one.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-stochastic-eigenvalue-bound">
      <title>Eigenvalues of a Stochastic Matrix Satisfy <m>|\lambda| \leq 1</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that eigenvalues of a stochastic matrix <m>A</m> satisfy
          <m>|\lambda| \leq 1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Assume <m>\vec{x} = \begin{bmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \\ \vdots \\ x_n \end{bmatrix}</m>
          is an eigenvector of <m>A^T</m> with eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m>, and assume that
          <m>x_i</m> is the entry of <m>\vec{x}</m> with largest absolute value, so
          <m>|x_j| \leq |x_i|</m> for all <m>j</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Writing <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} a_{ij} \end{bmatrix}</m> so that
          <m>A^T = \begin{bmatrix} a_{ji} \end{bmatrix}</m>, and focusing on the <m>i</m>th
          entry of <m>\lambda \vec{x} = A^T\vec{x}</m>,
          <md>
            \left| \lambda x_i \right| = \left| \sum_j a_{ji} x_j \right|
            \leq \sum_j \left| a_{ji} x_j \right|
            = \sum_j a_{ji} |x_j|
            \leq \underbrace{\sum_j a_{ji}}_{= \, 1} |x_i| = |x_i|,
          </md>
          where the first inequality holds because the absolute value of a sum is at most
          the sum of absolute values, and the last step uses that <m>A</m> is stochastic.
        </p>
        <p>
          Therefore <m>|\lambda x_i| \leq |x_i|</m> and hence <m>|\lambda| \leq 1</m>.
          Finally, since <m>A</m> and <m>A^T</m> have the same eigenvalues, we have
          completed the proof.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-steady-state">
    <title>Existence of a Unique Steady State</title>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-unique-steady-state">
      <title>Existence of a Unique Steady State</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If the transition matrix <m>T</m> of a Markov chain is a regular stochastic
          matrix, then it has a unique steady state.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-finding-steady-state">
      <title>Finding the Steady State</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If an <m>n \times n</m> regular matrix <m>T</m> is the transition matrix of a
          Markov chain, then the steady state vector can be found by solving
          <m>S = TS</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <example xml:id="eg-steady-state-alice">
      <title>The Steady State for Alice's Lunch</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Does the matrix
          <m>T = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp \frac{1}{4} \\ 1 \amp \frac{3}{4} \end{bmatrix}</m>
          have a unique steady state vector? If so, find the steady state vector.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(I)</term> <m>T</m> is a stochastic matrix, since the sum of entries in
          each column is equal to one.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(II)</term> <m>T</m> is a regular matrix, since
          <m>T^2 = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{1}{4} \amp \frac{3}{16} \\ \frac{3}{4} \amp \frac{13}{16} \end{bmatrix}</m>
          and all the entries of <m>T^2</m> are positive.
        </p>
        <p>
          From (I) and (II), <m>T</m> has a unique steady state vector.
        </p>
        <p>
          To find it, solve <m>T\vec{S} = \vec{S}</m>, or <m>(T - I)\vec{S} = \vec{0}</m>,
          for <m>\vec{S} = \begin{bmatrix} a \\ b \end{bmatrix}</m>:
          <md>
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
              -1 \amp \frac{1}{4} \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp -\frac{1}{4} \amp 0
            \end{array} \right]
            \longrightarrow
            \left[ \begin{array}{cc|c}
              1 \amp -\frac{1}{4} \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
            \end{array} \right]
            \; \Longrightarrow \;
            \vec{S} = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{t}{4} \\ t \end{bmatrix}, \quad t \in \R.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Now, remember that entries of the state <m>\vec{S}</m> represent Alice's
          probabilities of eating at restaurants <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, hence they must
          add up to one:
          <md>
            \frac{t}{4} + t = 1
            \; \longrightarrow \;
            \frac{5t}{4} = 1
            \; \longrightarrow \;
            t = \frac{4}{5},
          </md>
          so the steady state is
          <md>
            \vec{S} = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{1}{5} \\ \frac{4}{5} \end{bmatrix}
            = \begin{bmatrix} 0.2 \\ 0.8 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          Note that this is the same result that we got numerically earlier in
          <xref ref="eg-alice-lunch" text="type-global" />.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-two-by-two-steady-state-proof">
      <title>Why the Steady State Is Reached</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Prove the following statement: if <m>T</m> is a <m>2 \times 2</m> regular
          transition matrix then <m>T</m> has a steady state vector <m>\vec{x}_*</m> and
          furthermore for any initial vector <m>\vec{x}_0</m> we have
          <m>\lim_{n \to \infty} T^n \vec{x}_0 = \vec{x}_*</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          A <m>2 \times 2</m> stochastic matrix is of the form
          <md>
            T = \begin{bmatrix} a \amp 1 - b \\ 1 - a \amp b \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad \text{where } 0 \leq a \leq 1 \text{ and } 0 \leq b \leq 1.
          </md>
          Note that if <m>a = b = 0</m>, then
          <m>T = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}</m> and
          <m>T^n</m> alternates between <m>T</m> and <m>I</m>, hence <m>T</m> is not
          regular. Also, if <m>a = b = 1</m>, then
          <m>T = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, which is clearly
          not regular. Therefore, we can ignore these two cases.
        </p>
        <p>
          To compute <m>T^n</m>, we first diagonalize <m>T</m>. Computing the eigenvalues
          and the eigenvectors gives
          <md>
            \lambda_1 = 1, \quad \lambda_2 = a + b - 1,
            \qquad
            \vec{x}_1 = \begin{bmatrix} b - 1 \\ a - 1 \end{bmatrix},
            \quad \vec{x}_2 = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
          Then
          <md>
            T^n = PD^nP^{-1}
            = \begin{bmatrix} b-1 \amp -1 \\ a-1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}
              \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp (a+b-1)^n \end{bmatrix}
              \frac{1}{a+b-2} \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 1-a \amp b-1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Now <m>\lim_{n \to \infty} (a+b-1)^n = 0</m>, since <m>0 \lt a + b \lt 2</m> and
          therefore <m>-1 \lt a + b - 1 \lt 1</m>. Therefore
          <md>
            \lim_{n \to \infty} T^n
            = \frac{1}{a+b-2} \begin{bmatrix} b-1 \amp b-1 \\ a-1 \amp a-1 \end{bmatrix}.
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Now, if the initial probability vector is
          <m>\vec{x}_0 = \begin{bmatrix} p \\ 1-p \end{bmatrix}</m>, then
          <md>
            \lim_{n \to \infty} T^n \vec{x}_0
            = \frac{1}{a+b-2} \begin{bmatrix} b-1 \amp b-1 \\ a-1 \amp a-1 \end{bmatrix}
              \begin{bmatrix} p \\ 1-p \end{bmatrix}
            = \frac{1}{a+b-2} \begin{bmatrix} b-1 \\ a-1 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          which is the probability vector corresponding to eigenvector <m>\vec{x}_1</m>
          with eigenvalue one, and is independent of <m>p</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>Summary.</term>
          <ol marker="I">
            <li><p><m>T_{2 \times 2}</m> is a regular stochastic matrix.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>\vec{x}_0</m> is an initial probability vector.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\vec{x}_*</m> is a probability eigenvector of <m>T</m> corresponding to
                eigenvalue one.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                The steady state vector is <m>\vec{x}_*</m>:
                <m>\lim_{n \to \infty} T^n \vec{x}_0 = \vec{x}_*</m>. This means that to
                find the steady state vector of <m>T_{2 \times 2}</m>, we can find the
                eigenvector corresponding to eigenvalue one and normalize it.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
